-# Roadmap
-
-## Terms
-
-- The program is `phial`.
-- The host runs the program.
-- The guest is the virtual machine that the host runs.
-- Env mode describes a secret that the guest reads from a file.
-- Proxy mode describes a secret that the host injects into an outbound request.
-- A grant is a time box on one secret.
-
-## Threat model
-
-- The host is trusted, and the guest is not.
-- Only the host user adds a provider, removes a provider, or grants access.
-- The guest agent cannot add a provider. It may only use what the host already granted.
-- Leave room for a future request path, where the guest agent asks the host for a grant and the host answers.
-- One grant covers one SSH key, one GnuPG key, one API key, or one other secret.
-- Revocation at the provider is out of scope. Removing the local file is not revocation at the provider.
-
-## Host matrix
-
-- The host runs Fedora Workstation 45 during development.
-- The host takes QEMU and waypipe from DNF for now.
-- At the target, the host runs GNOME OS and takes the user space tools from Homebrew.
-- The guest runs Fedora Workstation 45.
-- The guest installs from a kickstart and boots with UEFI and Secure Boot.
-
-## Host toolchain
-
-- Install the user space tools with Homebrew, so one manifest works on both hosts.
-- Homebrew covers virtiofsd, passt, Caddy, socat, GnuPG, swtpm, FreeRDP, wl-clipboard, and zenity.
-- Take QEMU and waypipe from DNF on the development host.
-- Make sure that Fedora builds QEMU with virglrenderer.
-- Fedora also ships a GNOME pinentry and KeePassXC.
-- Record a note for each tool that Homebrew does not provide on Linux.
-- Keep the KeePassXC window open and unlocked, so its Secret Service answers one `keepassxc://` lookup with no prompt.
-
-## Historical notes
-
-- [QEMU and VM arguments](https://gist.github.com/pojntfx/b860e123e649504bcd298aa6e92c4043) holds the raw QEMU flags from earlier work.
-- [Headless virgl and waypipe](https://gist.github.com/pojntfx/32e13d3eda90f34a56913e9fb53e7ac3) holds the vsock and waypipe flow from earlier work.
-- Read both before you change the launcher or the display path.
-
-## Host acceptance
-
-- Run `qemu-system-x86_64 -display help`, and make sure that it lists `egl-headless`.
-- Run `qemu-system-x86_64 -device virtio-vga-gl,help`, and make sure that it lists `venus` and `drm_native_context`.
-- Run `qemu-system-x86_64 -device help`, and make sure that it lists `qemu-xhci`, `vhost-vsock-pci`, `vhost-user-fs-pci`, and `virtio-blk-pci`.
-- Run `qemu-system-x86_64 -machine q35,help`, and make sure that it lists `smm` and `kernel-irqchip`.
-- Run `virtiofsd --version`, and make sure that it reports the Rust backend.
-- Run `virt-fw-vars --help`, and make sure that it lists a DER enrollment flag.
-
-## VM launch
-
-- Write one shell script that builds the QEMU argument list in code.
-- Put the script in `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME`, because it is user configuration.
-- Open the script with `xdg-open`, so it loads in the default editor.
-- Set the CPU count, the memory size, the disk path, the display mode, and the USB list as variables at the top of the script.
-- Turn Secure Boot on by default, and add one switch to turn it off as an escape hatch.
-- Run the VM from one systemd user unit, and let the unit call the script.
-- Put the unit in `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/systemd/user`.
-- Read the runtime paths from `$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR`, and keep sockets and state there.
-- Add one desktop entry that opens the unit in the default editor.
-- Add one desktop entry that opens the script in the default editor.
-- Follow the XDG base directory rules for every path.
-- Use q35 with KVM, and give the guest the host CPU model.
-- Size the vCPU count to physical cores, and leave one core for the host.
-- Set the topology with `-smp <n>,sockets=1,cores=<n>,threads=1`.
-- Back the guest RAM with a shared memfd, and turn on huge pages.
-- Reserve huge pages with `vm.nr_hugepages` in `/etc/sysctl.d`.
-- Raise `LimitMEMLOCK` to infinity in the unit, because `mem-lock=on` needs it.
-- Split the IRQ chip with `kernel-irqchip=split`, because the VFIO devices need interrupt remapping.
-- Boot the guest with UEFI and Secure Boot.
-- Use the OVMF secboot code and vars files from the host.
-- Start swtpm before QEMU, and expose it on a control socket.
-- Add the TPM 2.0 device with `tpm-crb`.
-- Copy the OVMF vars template to a runtime path before the first boot.
-- Make sure that the firmware descriptor declares `requires-smm`, and keep `smm=on`.
-- Create the raw image with `qemu-img create -f raw -o preallocation=falloc`.
-- Add the raw disk with `cache=none,aio=native` and `discard=unmap`.
-- Give the disk an iothread and `num-queues=4`, and pin the iothread.
-- Add the vsock device with `guest-cid=3`.
-- Add the guest agent on a virtio serial port.
-- Add the two virtiofs devices, one per share.
-- Add the serial console in every run.
-- Keep the RTC on the host clock.
-- Run custom modules in the guest only after you enroll a Machine Owner Key (MOK).
-- Enroll a MOK with `mokutil` at boot, or with `virt-fw-vars` while the VM is shut down.
-- Use a run with a visible screen when MokManager needs it for enrollment.
-- Fall back to the Secure Boot off switch only when a MOK cannot be enrolled.
-- Acceptance: the guest reaches the firmware, then the installer.
-
-## Guest install
-
-- Install Fedora Workstation 45 from the netinst ISO.
-- Pass the kickstart file to the installer, so the first boot is unattended.
-- Select the workstation environment in the kickstart, so GNOME and the portals are present.
-- Create the user in the kickstart, and set the guest hostname to `phial`.
-- Install the SSH authorized keys from `https://github.com/pojntfx.keys` in the kickstart.
-- Install and enable `qemu-guest-agent`, so the agent channel answers from the first boot.
-- Run every later guest change as Ansible over that SSH entry.
-- Keep the guest as one pet machine, and let Ansible run again at any time.
-- Follow the Ansible layout of [vanlug-infrastructure](https://codeberg.org/pojntfx/vanlug-infrastructure), but use kickstart instead of cloud-init.
-- Enable nested virtualization in the guest, so driver tests can run in a nested VM.
-- Keep the kickstart for the parts that must exist at first boot.
-- Keep Ansible for everything else.
-- Acceptance: the guest boots under Secure Boot, and `ssh phial` reaches it.
-
-## Control plane
-
-- Add one guest unit that exposes SSH over vsock with socat.
-- Listen on vsock and forward to SSH on the guest loopback.
-- Add the host SSH entry, so `ssh phial` reaches the guest with no network device.
-- Expose one guest port to the host with one unit per port, so the host reaches a guest service.
-- Expose only the proxy port and the two agent sockets to the guest, so the guest reaches the host proxy and the granted agents, and nothing else.
-- Keep the agent forwards down until a grant opens, so the guest sees no agent socket outside a window.
-- Never forward the host loopback in general.
-- Bind the guest listeners to the IPv6 loopback, and add that address as the host alias `host.internal`.
-- Make the loopback alias IPv6 first, and use dual-stack only as a fallback.
-- Keep the IPv4 rule local to the loopback alias. The guest still dials IPv4 on the LAN and in an IPv4-only café.
-- Acceptance: SSH works with no NIC other than loopback.
-
-## Shares
-
-- Run one virtiofsd unit per share, and keep the sockets in the runtime directory.
-- Add one virtiofs device per share in the launcher.
-- Give the two shares the tags `hostshare` and `phial-secrets`.
-- Mount the secrets share with `ro,nosuid,nodev,noexec`.
-- Map the guest user to the host user on the secrets share, so the guest user reads the files and no other guest user does.
-- Set one explicit uid and gid map in virtiofsd, and do not rely on two matching defaults.
-- Acceptance: a host file appears in the guest with no restart.
-## Secrets store
-
-- Create one runtime secret directory on a tmpfs, with mode 0700.
-- Keep one mapping file, and give it four columns: name, mode, source, and upstream.
-- Resolve one source scheme: `keepassxc://`.
-- Read one entry through the KeePassXC Secret Service, so the KeePassXC window must be open and unlocked.
-- Write one file per secret into the secrets share.
-- Name each file after the secret, so the guest can source it as an environment variable.
-- Name one mode per row. `env` puts the secret in a file that the guest reads. `proxy` injects the secret into an outbound request on the host.
-- Send `proxy` rows to the host over the vsock proxy.
-- The host runs Caddy as a user unit, and Caddy injects the secret into the outbound request header before the request leaves.
-- Write every value with a temp file and a rename, so no reader sees a partial value.
-- Keep the mapping file in the user configuration directory, and open it with the default editor.
-- Expose one command that lists the secret names, and never the values.
-- Keep the SSH agent and the GnuPG agent out of the secret store.
-- Acceptance: the list command prints names and never values.
-
-## Grants
-
-- Add one command that grants one secret for a set duration.
-- Write the secret file, then touch one grant file that records the expiry.
-- Schedule the expiry with `systemd-run --on-active=<duration>`.
-- Add one command that revokes a grant and removes both files.
-- Add one command that reads the expiry and prints the remaining time.
-- Send a desktop notification on grant and on revoke.
-- Decide the exact grant subject types during implementation: an API key, an SSH key, or a GnuPG key.
-- Acceptance: the secret file disappears on time with no other step.
-## CLI and runner
-
-- Add one sync command that resolves every mapping row and writes every secret file.
-- Add one guest runner that loads every file in the secrets share as an environment variable, then runs the given command.
-- In the guest, load the secrets into guest units with `LoadCredential=` when a guest unit must not see the environment.
-- Add one path unit on the guest mount, and let it restart the units that consume secrets.
-- Keep the CLI as one program with the subcommands `sync`, `allow`, `revoke`, `status`, and `ls`.
-- Put the program in the user path, so the guest runner and the units can call it.
-- Acceptance: the guest runner prints the variable in the guest.
-## Agent forwarding
-
-- Run one dedicated SSH agent on the host, and keep its socket in the runtime directory.
-- Load only the guest keys into that agent.
-- Load each key with `ssh-add -c`, so the agent asks before it uses the key.
-- Load each key with `ssh-add -t 1h`, so the agent drops it after one hour.
-- Do not set `-h` for now. Accept that the forwarded key can sign for any host that already trusts its public key.
-- Rely on the public key itself for the boundary. Add it only to the systems that should accept it.
-- Run one plain `ssh-agent` as the dedicated agent, and point `SSH_ASKPASS` at a GTK askpass, so the confirm prompt appears on the host.
-- Forward the agent socket to the guest over vsock with one socat pair.
-- Forward the GnuPG extra socket to the guest over vsock with one socat pair.
-- Run `pinentry-gnome3` on the host for the GnuPG prompt, so the prompt appears there and not in the guest.
-- Keep no private key in the guest.
-- Acceptance: a guest push prompts on the host, and the guest holds no private key.
-## Proxy
-
-- Run Caddy on the host as a user unit.
-- Listen on one port on the host, and reach it from the guest as `http://host.internal:4000`.
-- Add `admin off` to the global options in the Caddyfile, so no control endpoint listens on the host.
-- Point Caddy at one directory of drop-in files, and let the sync command rewrite the main Caddyfile.
-- Apply the change with `systemctl --user reload phial-caddy`, and set `ExecReload` in the unit to send the reload signal.
-- Make sure that Caddy is 2.11 or newer, because `SIGUSR1` reloads only from that version. Restart the unit on an older build.
-- Do not rely on `--watch`, because Caddy watches the main config file and not the files it imports.
-- Write one drop-in file per proxy row when you add a provider.
-- Remove the drop-in file when you revoke the row.
-- Match the grant file for the row before Caddy injects the secret.
-- Inject the secret into the `Authorization` header of the outbound request.
-- Treat a bearer API key as the common case, and plan for one custom provider per service when that header is not enough.
-- Expect API keys for most providers, and add a drop-in and a mapping row for each custom one.
-- Keep the secret on the host for every provider that allows it, so the guest holds only a placeholder.
-- Put the secret in the guest only when host-side injection cannot work, for example an OIDC flow or a client that signs its own requests.
-- Avoid that path. It is the exception, and it gives the guest the real value.
-- Respond with 403 when the grant file is missing.
-- Add one default handler that answers 404, so an unmatched path returns 404 on purpose.
-- Point the guest at the host path that names the row, and use a placeholder token in the guest.
-- Acceptance: revoking the grant returns 403 on the next request.
-
-## Display and audio
-
-- Add the virtio GPU with virglrenderer, and turn on blob, venus, and the native context.
-- Run one egl-headless display, so the guest draws without a window.
-- Run a persistent waypipe server in the guest and a waypipe client on the host over vsock.
-- Set WAYLAND_DISPLAY=0 and XDG_SESSION_TYPE=wayland in the guest, so guest apps render on the host.
-- Use the guest desktop database to list and launch apps by real name, with a CLI command as fallback.
-- Use host-spawn in the Flatpak to open terminals on the host for serial console access.
-- Run gnome-remote-desktop for the full session, and send the desktop over RDP.
-- Send audio over RDP, so no extra QEMU audio device is needed.
-- Send the clipboard over RDP as well.
-- Use PipeWire in the guest, and leave the QEMU audio device out.
-- Add an audio device only for a run that cannot use RDP audio.
-- Add a GTK display to the launcher variables, and swap it on when RDP is not enough.
-- Use the GTK display for early boot, for the firmware, and for MokManager.
-- Swap the display on temporarily from the desktop file based configurator, so one switch changes the run.
-- Add a serial console to every run, so you can debug the kernel when you need to.
-- Acceptance: `gnome-shell` runs on the virtio GPU, and one GL app appears through waypipe.
-## Networking
-
-- Create one bridge on the host, and add the Ethernet port to it.
-- Add one tap to the bridge, and let one user own it.
-- Allow the forward path on the bridge in the host firewall.
-- Pass one USB Wi-Fi adapter to the guest, so the guest owns the wireless path.
-- Make mDNS work on the Wi-Fi adapter as well as on the bridge.
-- Run one passt instance as the fallback path, and give that route the highest guest route metric.
-- Start passt from QEMU with `-nic passt,model=virtio-net-pci`, so QEMU auto-starts it and no socket is needed.
-- Make sure that the QEMU build lists `passt` in `-netdev help`, and keep a `-netdev stream` socket as the fallback.
-- Let passt send the guest traffic out of whatever host device the host route picks.
-- Use the same passt instance for the host WLAN and the host WWAN.
-- Prefer IPv6 on every interface, and use dual-stack only as a fallback.
-- Acceptance: a scan from the guest sees the LAN, and mDNS resolves both ways.
-## Hardware passthrough
-
-- Pass named USB devices with `usb-host`, and name each one by vendor and product ID.
-- Pass one whole USB hub to the guest with `vfio-pci` on a second xHCI controller.
-- Attach any USB device to that hub, for example a fastboot device or a UART adapter.
-- Never bind the only xHCI controller to the guest.
-- Keep the guest serial console for guest kernel work.
-- List the devices to forward in the launcher variables, so you can change them without editing the code.
-- Use `kernel-irqchip=split` on the host, because the VFIO devices need interrupt remapping.
-- Acceptance: three unplug and replug cycles return every device to the guest.
-## Performance
-
-- Use q35, because it is the newest x86 machine type and there is no newer one.
-- Give the guest the host CPU model, so the guest sees every host feature.
-- Size the vCPU count to physical cores, and leave one core for the host.
-- Back the guest RAM with the shared memfd, and turn on huge pages.
-- Create the raw image preallocated, and use `cache=none,aio=native` on it.
-- Keep the RTC on the host clock.
-- No GPU passes through. Use venus on the virtio GPU, so the guest shares the host GPU over virtio.
-- Size the CPU and the RAM first, because heavy work still uses them.
-- Measure before you tune. Use `perf` in the guest and `perf kvm` on the host.
-
-## Guest lifecycle
-
-- Install the guest once from the kickstart.
-- Update the guest with Ansible, and treat it as a mutable pet.
-- Publish no image, and build no base image.
-## Guest tooling
-
-- Install the base system with the kickstart, and add nothing else.
-- Leave every tool to the user.
-- Enable nested virtualization, so driver tests can run in a nested VM.
-- Run one nested GNOME session for shell and extension work.
-- Test the portals in the nested session, because the guest owns that session.
-- Use waypipe by default for desktop apps, and switch to RDP when you need shell and session integration.
-
-## Agent skills
-
-- Write one global skill set in the guest, and distribute it with Ansible.
-- Tell each guest agent how the whole system works, so it can drive the VM and consume the secrets that the host granted.
-- Name every feature the agent can use: the launcher, the runner, the proxy, and the display paths.
-- Keep the sync command and the grant commands off the guest agent. Only the host user adds a provider or grants access.
-- Say which secrets must stay secret, and say that the agent must never print a value.
-- Point the agent at the guest serial console for kernel work.
-- Point the agent at the waypipe path for single apps and at the RDP path for the full session.
-- Acceptance: a guest agent can consume a granted secret for an hour, and it cannot add a provider or extend a grant.
-
-## Shell removal
-
-- Remove every shell script except the kickstart and the systemd units.
-- A script that must exist becomes a small Go subcommand or a function in the main binary.
-- pinentry-gnome3 replaces the GTK askpass script for ssh-add confirmation prompts.
-- The acceptance run, the installer, the root setup, the vfio helper, the waypipe helper, the RDP helper, the guest forward dispatcher, and the guest runner all become Go.
-- Acceptance: the tree holds no .sh files outside guest/phial.ks and the unit files.
-
-## Host application
-
-- Ship one static Go binary with no cgo.
-- Build the GUI with puregotk and libadwaita. Follow the GNOME Human Interface Guidelines.
-- Use a sidebar for top level navigation. Use boxed lists for settings lists. Use header bar buttons for page actions.
-- Use action dialogs to create items. Use placeholders for empty states. Use toasts for non-critical errors. Use confirmation only for irreversible actions.
-- The GUI drives one MCP daemon for every state change. It never edits state files behind the daemon.
-- One systemd user unit runs the daemon. The daemon serves MCP over Streamable HTTP on a unix socket in the runtime directory.
-- The guest reaches the same MCP over the vsock forward as http://host.internal:8080/mcp.
-- The MCP tools cover every feature: secrets, grants, virtual machines, forwards, USB, display, approvals, and system setup status.
-- Acceptance: one static binary runs the GUI, and the daemon serves every MCP tool.
-
-## Requests and approvals
-
-- The guest agent calls the MCP on the host for actions, for example a new redirect or a grant request.
-- Actions that add a provider or open a grant wait for host approval. The threat model from the earlier phase applies.
-- The GUI lists pending requests with Approve and Deny controls. A desktop notification announces each new request.
-- Do not show a dialog without a user action. Show the request in the requests view and in a notification.
-- A pending request expires after five minutes and then counts as denied.
-- Acceptance: a guest request appears in the GUI and in a notification, and the grant opens only after the host approves.
-
-## Secrets with KeePassXC
-
-- Keep the Secret Service as the store. The GUI resolves keepassxc:// rows for display names and never shows values.
-- The GUI can write a test entry into the store, so the store path gets exercised end to end.
-- Add, edit, and remove providers through action dialogs in the GUI.
-- Acceptance: a provider created in the GUI resolves through the Secret Service and grants from the GUI.
-
-## Virtual machines
-
-- Hold multiple VM definitions as JSON files in $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/phial/vms.
-- One definition carries the name, the CPU count, the memory size, the disk path and size, the display mode, the GPU mode, the Secure Boot switch, the USB list, the xHCI controller, and the network mode.
-- The GUI manages the lifecycle: create, start, stop, restart, install from the kickstart, and delete with confirmation.
-- The launcher builds the QEMU argument list in Go per definition. The template unit phial-vm@.service runs the VM in the foreground.
-- Manage USB in the GUI: the named device list, the second xHCI controller selection, and bind or restore through run0.
-- Manage display in the GUI: the mode switch, the GPU mode, waypipe app launch, RDP open, and serial attach.
-- Acceptance: two VMs with different definitions run at the same time, and their USB and display settings apply.
-
-## Flatpak
-
-- Ship one manifest, com.pojtinger.felicitas.Phial.json, on the GNOME Platform runtime with the Go SDK extension.
-- Build every dependency into the Flatpak: the app, QEMU, virtiofsd, passt, swtpm, socat, Caddy, FreeRDP, waypipe, libsecret, and edk2 for the OVMF firmware.
-- Grant wayland, x11 fallback, ipc, dri, kvm, device all for vfio and vhost-vsock, network share, session bus, system bus for systemd and polkit, and the secrets and notifications talk names.
-- Keep system setup host side. Huge pages, udev rules, the bridge, and the DNF tools need root. The GUI offers one button that runs run0 for them, so polkit asks the user.
-- Write the submission metadata: metainfo.xml, the desktop file, icon.svg and icon-symbolic.svg, and the doap file.
-- Acceptance: the manifest passes flatpak-builder-lint, and the app builds and runs from the Flatpak.
-
-## Data locations and portals
-
-- Keep definitions and the mapping in $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/phial.
-- Keep disks and install assets in $XDG_DATA_HOME/phial.
-- Keep sockets, the grants, the secrets share, the MCP socket, and the runtime state in $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/phial.
-- Use portals whenever one exists for the job: notifications, the file chooser, and the screenshot portal.
-- Use direct D-Bus for systemd and for the Secret Service, because no portal covers them.
