diff --git a/docs/DESIGN.md b/docs/DESIGN.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..eda972c316b28c965c02d714282ce55dd9db6d7d
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/DESIGN.md
@@ -0,0 +1,282 @@
+# Design
+
+This document records the interface design of the Phial application and the reason
+for each decision. The rules come from the GNOME Human Interface Guidelines.
+
+## The window
+
+The window holds one sidebar and one content area.
+
+```
+AdwApplicationWindow
+└── AdwToastOverlay
+    └── AdwNavigationSplitView          collapsed below 550sp
+        ├── sidebar  AdwNavigationPage
+        │   └── AdwToolbarView
+        │       ├── AdwHeaderBar        New Machine, Main Menu
+        │       └── AdwSidebar          Machines, Providers, Requests
+        └── content  AdwNavigationPage
+            └── AdwNavigationView       one page per machine, per provider, and the
+                                        Providers and Requests pages
+```
+
+The sidebar holds the machine list, because a user switches between machines often.
+The navigation guideline allows a sidebar for dynamic locations and for frequent
+switching.
+
+The breakpoint at 550sp collapses the split view. The sidebar then becomes the first
+page of the stack and the content area follows it. The header bar of the content
+area carries the back control.
+
+A page switch names the pages of the new stack, and the widget removes every other
+page of the view. The application adds the cached pages back before each switch, so
+that a later switch finds them.
+
+## The sidebar
+
+The sidebar carries one section for the machines and one section for the tools.
+
+- A machine row carries the hard disk icon, the name, and the state as a subtitle.
+- The Providers row carries the key icon, the count as a subtitle, and a badge with
+  the number of open grants.
+- The Requests row carries the inbox icon, the pending count as a subtitle, and a
+  badge with the same count.
+
+The New Machine control and the main menu sit in the header bar of the sidebar. The
+navigation guideline asks for sidebar controls above the list and for the main menu
+above the sidebar list.
+
+## The empty state
+
+A fresh installation carries no machine, and the window states that once.
+
+- The Machines section keeps its heading and carries one row, Get Started.
+- The row is selected, and it shows the empty page. The button on the page creates the machine.
+- The header shows no create control while the row is present, because the page carries the button.
+- The row opens no dialog, so the only control that creates a machine is the button.
+- The page carries the icon, the heading No Machines, one line of description, and one suggested pill button, New Machine.
+- The row leaves as soon as the first machine exists, and the header control returns with it.
+
+## The machine page
+
+The machine page is a settings style page with boxed lists. The order follows the
+questions that a user asks.
+
+1. **Installation** appears only while an install runs. It lists the seven install
+   steps and Cancel Installation. The current step carries the precise progress in its
+   subtitle, such as the downloaded size and the share of the download, or the package
+   name with its position in the package list.
+2. **Overview** carries the state and, while the machine runs, the live CPU, memory,
+   disk, network, and graphics figures.
+3. **Applications and Desktop** carries Guest Applications, Remote Desktop, Terminal,
+   Serial Console, and the SSH command. Each row opens a dialog or copies a command.
+4. **Shared Folders** lists the shared host directories and offers Share a Folder.
+5. **USB Devices** lists the passed devices and offers Add USB Device.
+6. **Whole USB Hub** offers one controller, or none.
+7. **Storage** carries the facts that no dialog changes: the disk image path, and a
+   control that shows the folder of the image in the file manager.
+
+Every value that a dialog can change lives in Machine Settings, and the page does not
+repeat it. The header bar carries one primary control, one pencil, and one menu:
+
+- The primary control is Install, Start, or Stop, and it follows the state of the machine.
+- The pencil opens Machine Settings.
+- The menu carries Logs, Provision the Machine, Restart, and Delete Machine.
+- Logs opens the log of the machine in the text editor: the console of the installer when it keeps one, else the service log of the machine.
+- The Serial Console and Remote Desktop dialogs state the user and the password with a copy control for each, because the guest asks for both at its login.
+- The Terminal and Remote Desktop dialogs carry Unlock the SSH Agent, which loads the key of the user into the dedicated agent of Phial and asks for the passphrase through pinentry.
+- Every command in the guest states a timeout, so a guest that stops answering states an error instead of holding the app.
+- The install provisions the machine: it boots the guest once, waits for the guest to answer, copies the units of the guest into it, and stops it again.
+- The guest turns plymouth off and masks the network wait, because a machine that waits for a splash or for the network never reaches a login.
+- The installer writes the home directory of the guest as root, so the provisioning repairs the labels of that directory before the session of the user writes its units.
+- A machine installs in minutes rather than seconds, so the first start of a fresh machine needs a few minutes before the guest answers.
+- The engine states the DRM native context of the host renderer, because a renderer without support for it leaves the guest without a working display.
+- The generated ssh configuration names no agent, so ssh uses the agent of the session, which asks for the passphrase of the key on the first use.
+
+Rules that the page keeps:
+
+- The page never shows an impossible control. Cancel Installation replaces the
+  primary control while an install runs, and the delete control stays insensitive.
+- The access rows become insensitive while the machine is stopped, because no entry
+  can reach a machine that does not run.
+- The page shows the disk path and the configuration as property rows, because a
+  property row carries a name and a value and it reflows at any width.
+
+## The Providers page and the provider page
+
+The Providers page carries one group of provider rows and one group with Sync
+Secrets Now. An empty list becomes a status page with an Add Provider control.
+
+A provider row carries the name, the mode, the source or the upstream, and the
+remaining grant time. A row opens the provider page.
+
+The provider page carries two groups:
+
+- **Secret** carries the mode, the source, and the upstream. The page never shows a
+  value, and the group description says so.
+- **Grant** carries the access state, Grant Access, and Revoke Access. The state
+  counts down while a grant is open.
+
+## The Forwards page
+
+The Forwards page carries one row for each forward. A forward is one socket that a
+machine reaches on this host.
+
+- A row carries the forward name, the listen address, the target address, and a
+  switch. The switch starts or stops the user service of the forward.
+- A remove control sits at the end of the row and asks for confirmation, because the
+  control deletes the configuration of the forward.
+- One Add Forward control sits at the end of the list.
+- The sidebar entry states the count and the number of running forwards, because a
+  stopped agent channel is the reason that an agent cannot reach the host.
+
+## The Requests page
+
+The Requests page holds the pending approvals. Each row carries the action, the
+detail, the age, and two controls, Deny and Approve.
+
+The page shows a status page while no request waits. The description states the
+five minute limit, so that a user knows what happens to an ignored request.
+
+A request also raises a notification with a Review action. The notification carries
+the user to this page.
+
+## States and long-running tasks
+
+Every state of a machine and every task that takes time has a control, a message,
+and an end. The application never shows a state that it cannot leave.
+
+### A machine
+
+| State | The page shows | The header control |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Not installed | State "Not installed", no live usage | Install |
+| Installing | The step list with the progress of the current step, Cancel Installation | A spinner, no control, and an insensitive pencil |
+| Installed and stopped | State "Stopped", no live usage | Start |
+| Starting | State "Starting", a spinner in the header | Start, insensitive |
+| Running, guest not up | State "Starting", the usage rows | Stop |
+| Running and ready | State "Running", the usage rows, the access rows on | Stop |
+| Stopping | State "Stopping", a spinner in the header | Stop, insensitive |
+| Restarting | State "Restarting" | The control, insensitive |
+
+A transition clears when the machine reaches the new state, or after three minutes.
+The header control stays insensitive while a transition runs, so a second press
+cannot start a second transition.
+
+### The installation
+
+The installation carries seven steps. Each step carries one of four appearances:
+done with a checkmark, current with a spinner, pending and dimmed, or failed with the
+error as its subtitle.
+
+The current step carries the progress, so the page needs no second control:
+
+- The download step states the downloaded size, the full size, and the share, such as "381.0 MiB of 1.2 GiB, 32%".
+- The package step states the package name and its position, such as "kernel-core, 96 of 412 packages".
+- Every other step states the message of the installer.
+
+Cancel Installation asks for confirmation and stops the installer. A failed step keeps
+the error on screen, and the step states the reason that the virtual machine gave,
+such as a failed memory mapping.
+
+### The network
+
+A machine reaches the network through one of two paths, and both must work.
+
+- The LAN path uses the bridge and its tap device, and Phial adds the tap only when the bridge carries a link. A network device without a link stops the guest network from coming up at all, so the automatic setting leaves it out on a Wi-Fi host.
+- The fallback path runs passt, which serves DHCP, the resolver 192.168.100.1, and the traffic of the guest.
+- passt forwards the queries of the guest to the resolver of the host. It reaches the stub resolver of systemd-resolved at 127.0.0.53 when it exists, because the stub also handles the translation that an IPv6-only host needs.
+
+### The installer
+
+The installer runs on its own devices, so the settings of the machine cannot stop it.
+
+- The installer uses one console, the serial console, so its text interface and its messages reach the console log that Phial reads.
+- The installer uses a plain display device, because the accelerated devices can stop the guest kernel.
+- The installer turns the graphical boot screen off, because the boot otherwise waits for a screen that never appears.
+- The host network settings reach the installer through the kickstart file, such as the address of the resolver.
+
+### The host resources
+
+The host may not be able to serve what a machine asks for, so Phial states the
+facts and keeps the machine usable:
+
+- Machine Settings states the huge pages of the host, such as "12.5 GiB free of 16.5 GiB reserved, too little for this machine".
+- A machine whose memory does not fit the free huge pages runs on normal pages instead.
+- A machine whose memory does not fit the free memory runs unlocked instead, so it stays swappable.
+- Both steps are written to the log with the machine name and the size that was asked for.
+
+### The live usage
+
+The usage appears while the machine runs: CPU in cores, memory, the disk space on this
+host, the network rate, and the graphics mode. Each row carries a defined empty state:
+
+- The machine does not run: one dimmed row states that no live usage exists.
+- The guest agent does not answer: the network row states this.
+- No traffic: the network row states this.
+- The metrics fail: one dimmed row reports the failure.
+
+### The secret sync
+
+The Providers page carries one Sync Secrets control. The control reports the result as
+a toast, and the grant list refreshes on its own.
+
+### The requests
+
+A request waits for an answer. The Requests page states the five minute limit in its
+empty state, and the sidebar badge counts the waiting requests.
+
+### The forwards
+
+A forward runs or it does not. The switch reports the state of the user service, and a
+failed start returns the switch to the real state.
+
+## Dialogs
+
+A dialog carries a short form or one decision. The application uses a dialog for
+these tasks:
+
+- Create a machine, edit a machine, add or edit a provider, grant access, add a USB
+  device, and share a folder.
+- Show the guest applications, the remote desktop, the terminal, and the serial
+  console.
+- Report the system setup and the acceptance report.
+- Confirm a delete, a cancel, and a quit.
+
+A dialog carries the Cancel control first and the affirmative control last. The
+affirmative control stays insensitive until the form is valid. The Escape key closes
+the dialog.
+
+## Keyboard and accessibility
+
+- Ctrl+N creates a machine, Ctrl+R syncs the secrets, Ctrl+Comma opens the
+  preferences, Ctrl+Question opens the shortcuts, Ctrl+W closes the window, and
+  Ctrl+Q quits.
+- Ctrl+1 to Ctrl+9 select the first nine machines.
+- Every control carries a label or an accessible tooltip.
+- Every list row carries a title and, where it helps, a subtitle.
+- The interface carries no color-only information. A state is always spelled out.
+- Custom styling stays at two badge classes that use the accent color variables.
+
+## Adaptive behavior
+
+- The split view collapses below 550sp.
+- Every page sits inside an AdwClamp with a maximum size of 600, so that lines stay
+  short on a wide window.
+- The content area holds property rows and boxed lists. Both reflow at any width.
+- The window keeps its size and its last page between runs, and it restores both.
+
+## Terms in the interface
+
+One term carries one meaning in the interface, the documentation, and the code.
+
+- A machine runs one Fedora guest. The command line calls it a virtual machine.
+- A provider names one secret in the password store.
+- A secret is the value that a provider names.
+- A grant is a time box on one secret.
+- A request is an agent's ask for a provider, a grant, or a device.
+- A forward carries one socket between a machine and this host. Its page carries the name Forwards.
+- A shared folder is a host folder that the machine mounts. The interface writes shared folders, never shares.
+- A USB device is one device that a machine claims. A USB hub is the whole hub.
+- This host is the computer that runs Phial.
+- An agent is a program inside a machine that asks the host for access.
